@page "/guide/climbing"

<GuidePage Title="Climbing Logbook guide" Path="/guide/climbing" Sections="Contents"
           Lede="Your ascents, crags, routes and partners in one place: filter the logbook, look for patterns across years and trips, and add new crags and routes from articles. Everything else works as in the general guide.">
    <Hero>
        <div class="d-flex flex-wrap mt-3" style="gap: 8px">
            <MudButton Href="/logbook" Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" StartIcon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.MenuBook">Open the logbook</MudButton>
            <MudButton Href="/guide" Variant="Variant.Outlined">General guide</MudButton>
        </div>
    </Hero>
    <ChildContent>
        <GuideSection Id="model" Title="What's in a logbook" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Terrain">
            <p>A logbook is made of six types, linked the way climbing actually works.</p>
            <div class="guide-types">
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #2563eb">Area</span>
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #a16207">Crag</span>
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #7c3aed">Route</span>
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #15803d">Ascent</span>
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #c2410c">Partner</span>
                <span class="guide-type" style="--chip: #be185d">Trip</span>
            </div>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><strong>Areas</strong> nest: a country holds regions, which hold smaller areas. <strong>Crags</strong> are in an area, and <strong>routes</strong> are at a crag.</li>
                <li>An <strong>ascent</strong> is you climbing a route on a day, with a style, notes and the partners who were there.</li>
                <li>A <strong>trip</strong> is a run of climbing days in a place. You never enter these: they are worked out from your ascents.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The Home page counts each type and every kind of link, so you can see at a glance what is loaded.</p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="logbook" Title="The logbook" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.MenuBook">
            <p>The Logbook page is every ascent, newest first, in a table you can sort by date, route, crag or style.</p>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><strong>Filter</strong> by date range, area, crag, grade system, discipline and style. Filters combine, and the grid and the summary both follow them.</li>
                <li><strong>Summary</strong> opens above the table with your hardest clean ascent in each grade system, a count by discipline and a count by year. Falls, hangdogs, seconds and top-rope climbs do not count as clean.</li>
                <li><strong>Add ascent</strong> records a new one. The pencil beside any row opens that ascent to edit.</li>
                <li>Each row shows the date, route, grade, crag, style, partners, trip and notes.</li>
            </ul>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="insights" Title="Insights" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.Insights">
            <p>Insights looks for patterns across your whole logbook, in five tabs.</p>
            <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Elevation="0" Class="mb-3">
                <thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Trips</td><td>Every trip abroad with its dates, days, crags and partners. Your biggest trips by climbing days, the countries you keep going back to, trips per year and two-day trips.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Places</td><td>Crags you return to year after year, how many crags in each country you visited once or five-plus days, where you repeat the most, and crags you have not visited for five years or more.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Seasons</td><td>Climbing days per month for every year, ascents per month by country, a season-by-season split and the mix of disciplines by year.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Partners</td><td>Who you climb with most, by trips and by ascents.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>On this day</td><td>What you were climbing on today's date in past years.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </MudSimpleTable>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="explore" Title="Exploring a logbook" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Hub">
            <p>
                The general guide explains Explore. These searches show what it is good for with climbing data. Choose the steps from the suggestions
                as you type, and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.
            </p>
            <div class="guide-phrase">
                <span class="pattern-node" style="--chip: #7c3aed; background: var(--chip)"><span class="pattern-node-label">Route</span><span class="pattern-node-filter">grade = "7a"</span></span>
            </div>
            <p class="guide-caption">All the routes graded 7a. Right-click one and expand to see the ascents on it, and who was with you.</p>
            <div class="guide-phrase">
                <span class="pattern-node" style="background: #7c3aed"><span class="pattern-node-label">Route</span></span>
                <span class="pattern-rel">← OF</span>
                <span class="pattern-node" style="background: #15803d"><span class="pattern-node-label">Ascent</span></span>
                <span class="pattern-rel">WITH →</span>
                <span class="pattern-node" style="background: #c2410c"><span class="pattern-node-label">Partner</span></span>
            </div>
            <p class="guide-caption">Routes, the ascents of them, and who you climbed with. The relationships are offered as you build the phrase.</p>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li>Type a partner's name as plain text, then expand them to see every trip and ascent you shared.</li>
                <li>Routes show their grade on the canvas, and ascents are captioned with date and style.</li>
                <li>Type <code>graph</code> to see all six types and how they link.</li>
            </ul>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="web" Title="Adding crags and routes from the web" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.CloudDownload">
            <p>
                The <em>Read text</em> tab of the Import page turns an article or a list into crags and routes, with you in control. Claude only reads the text and
                suggests facts. It needs your administrator to have set it up, and is described in the general guide.
            </p>
            <ol class="guide-list">
                <li><strong>Give it the text.</strong> Paste it, which is the most reliable way and the right one for sites that block fetching or forbid scraping. Or fetch a page by address, if the site allows it.</li>
                <li><strong>Review.</strong> Each crag, route and link found is listed. Untick what you do not want. Nothing is in the logbook yet. A crag or route that looks like one you already have is treated as that one, and the notes say so. A crag is only kept if the text says which area it is in.</li>
                <li><strong>Add.</strong> Only the ticked rows are written. Records that were already there are not changed, unless you tick the update box on the Read text tab: then a crag or route with exactly the same name gets the values the text gives (a route's grade, say), and you see each change first.</li>
                <li><strong>Undo.</strong> Recent imports are listed on the Import page, and an import can be undone later. Anything you have edited or logged ascents on since stays, and is listed.</li>
            </ol>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Dense="true" Class="mb-3">
                Only facts are kept: names, grades, lengths, locations and the source address. The page's own text is never stored.
            </MudAlert>
            <p>
                The answer is treated as untrusted. Any name or grade that is not in the text you gave is dropped, and the review page says what was removed.
            </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="files" Title="Loading your existing logbook" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.UploadFile">
            <p>
                Existing history comes from a UKC export and Mountain Project ticks. They are loaded all at once from the export files by whoever looks after
                the app, not from a page, so they are easy to repeat. Loading them again is safe: rows already imported are recognised and skipped.
            </p>
            <p>Each file is one import, so it shows under Recent imports and can be undone like any other.</p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="trips" Title="Trips, and why they can't be edited" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Flight">
            <p>
                Trips are worked out from your ascents and rebuilt whenever an ascent's date or country changes. Because of that, any hand edit to a trip
                would be lost at the next rebuild, so trip pages are read-only. To change a trip, change the ascents.
            </p>
            <p>Some trips span more than one country, so a few are merged by hand-kept corrections. If one looks wrong, ask whoever looks after the app to add a correction.</p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Id="shortcuts" Title="Shortcuts that help here" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Keyboard">
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><kbd>/</kbd> focuses the search box, or the first field on the Logbook page.</li>
                <li><kbd>g</kbd> then a number goes to that item in the rail: <kbd>1</kbd> Home, <kbd>2</kbd> Explore, <kbd>3</kbd> Logbook, <kbd>4</kbd> Import, <kbd>5</kbd> Insights.</li>
                <li>The full list is in the <MudLink Href="/guide#keyboard">general guide</MudLink>, or press <kbd>?</kbd>.</li>
            </ul>
        </GuideSection>
    </ChildContent>
</GuidePage>

@code {
    public static readonly IReadOnlyList<(string Id, string Title)> Contents =
    [
        ("model", "What's in a logbook"),
        ("logbook", "The logbook"),
        ("insights", "Insights"),
        ("explore", "Exploring a logbook"),
        ("web", "Adding crags and routes from the web"),
        ("files", "Loading your existing logbook"),
        ("trips", "Trips, and why they can't be edited"),
        ("shortcuts", "Shortcuts that help here"),
    ];
}
